/**

@page benchmarks_exahype2_euler_smooth_setup Convergence Tests (Euler Equations)

\tableofcontents

This is a simple benchmark used to perform convergence tests for a solver.
It uses the Euler equations to advance a 2D setup where the density follows a sine wave,
and the initial velocity is constant in the x direction. The domain is centred around the
origin.

## Compilation
Peano should be built with the following options: --enable-exahype, --enable-loadbalancing,
 --enable-blockstructured
The relevant mesh size and timestep size should be set in the python script directly. For 
the mesh size, $p$ should be modified in the expression $\frac{0.2}{p}$ where $p$ is the
number of cells that are desired in each coordinate direction.


## Postproccessing
The output after a run will be 2 snapshots of teh solution, one at the start and one at the
end (this can be modified), as Peano patch files. These can be converted to vtk files for 
visualisation in Paraview.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
     pvpython ../../../../python/peano4/visualisation/render.py --filter-fine-grid solution-CCZ4DG2RK2.peano-patch-file
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
The paraview module should be loaded where neccessary (system dependent) for this command.

In order to directly ocmpare results, my preferred method is to apply a plot over line filter
in Paraview, with fixed spacing between plot points such that they are always at the centre of
a cell for every resolution. This is converted to a csv file and read in Python using the csv
library. Plots can then be generated using this data.

*/
